/read and /plan now refuse bash deletes. The fix is committed as ca499d89 (Fixes #1192), and build, test, clippy and fmt all pass.

The bug: both modes say "read-only commands are allowed", but rm -rf src/ still ran. The read guard checks commands with two classifiers. The write classifier's verb list had no delete verbs, and the destructive classifier only flags system paths, so a delete of project files passed both.

The fix: I added rm, rmdir, unlink and shred to that verb list in src/safety.rs. The read guard is still the only production caller of the write classifier (I re-grepped), so changing the list fixes it there and nowhere else.

What the new tests show: src/read_guard_delete_tests.rs runs each command through the same two checks the guard runs. I ran it once before the fix and it failed as expected.
• Before: 12 of the 17 delete/write commands got through, including rm -rf src/, sudo rm, env rm, /bin/rm, rmdir, unlink, shred -u, echo $(rm x) and ls && rm x.
• Already refused before the fix: find -delete, dd of=, git clean -fdx, rsync --delete and git rm.
• After: all 17 are refused, plus the two existing cases (chmod, touch). All 8 commands that only mention a delete word, like grep -rn rm src/ and cat rmdir.txt, are still allowed.

Nothing was left over, so I didn't file a follow-up issue.

Old test: a_scratch_path_delete_is_not_a_hole_... asserted that rm -rf /tmp/... gets through both checks, which was the bug itself. I renamed it to a_scratch_path_delete_is_refused_as_a_write_with_or_without_a_prefix, made it assert the refusal, and kept the old verdict in its comment marked as superseded.

src/safety.rs grew from 4783 to 4789 lines; I updated its entry in tests/module_size.rs. No docs page lists what /read refuses, so there were no docs to change.
